A||||6||fogglethorpe||2001 music review||||5||
Z||000000||fogglethorpe||11-14-2001||09:20 PM||||  Music.<BR>   It's more than mere entertainment, or a way to pass idle time during <BR>elevator rides. It is a passion, a simple joy...<BR>   Music can be therapy, a personal statement, or a vehicle for evangelism<BR>or social change.<BR>   It can inform, nurture, inspire, or heal.<BR>   It brings cultures together, and splits generations apart.<BR>   It stirs emotion in the stoic, romance in the prudish, and reverence in<BR>the rebellious.<BR>   Three week old infants respond to it instinctively.<BR>   It can even rescue a bad movie.<BR>   Music has power.<BR>   Therefore, I have lovingly compiled a list of what I consider to be the best<BR>musical offerings of this year, in order, and with brief commentary.<P>1) "musicforthemorningafter" by Pete Yorn- Sometimes bombastic, sometimes <BR>gently acoustic, these songs start out simply, and build to irresistable, layered<BR>crescendos. He has a way with subtle melodies and hooks, which had me<BR>firmly in their grasp after a few listens.<P>2) "Drops of Jupiter" by Train- Brilliant and virtuosic, this album sounds heavily <BR>influenced by the big rock sounds of the 1970s, yet carries a contemporary,<BR>alternative edginess as well. This allows it to sound vibrant and have staying power.<BR>I do not exhaggerate when I say that the title track from this album is<BR>one of the most amazing pop-rock songs I have ever heard. It's a rare<BR>gem that's much more than the sum of it's parts.<P>3) "Amnesiac" by Radiohead- On this release, Radiohead has nearly abandoned<BR>traditional song structures. There are no verses, choruses, or bridges here...<BR>instead, it is a mangled, incohesive wash of sound. And for some strange reason,<BR>it's wonderful.<P>4) "Essence" by Lucinda Williams- She is arguably the best songwriter at the<BR>moment, male or female. The Zen-like simplicity of her lyrics, along with the<BR>spare production of the music, give these songs great emotional power. It's <BR>obvious that she is baring her soul without reservation.<P>5) "Americatown" by Five for Fighting- Though technically released last year,<BR>this album has come into prominence this year. It is a beautiful chronicle of<BR>life in small town America, tackling subjects like love, competitiveness, and<BR>stress in an intelligent, sometimes metaphorical manner. <P>I'll return later to post "honorable mention".<P>I'm interested in knowing what music has excited you this year.<BR>   ||||reg||5
Z||000001||AkursedX||11-14-2001||09:29 PM||||Well I would agree with your views on Radiohead's "Amnesiac"<P>The only CD that I thought was really good this year that I bought was Depeche Mode's "Exciter"  It just sounds like depeche Mode to me, and that is reason enough for me to love it.  I really can't give any better of a review than that.  I think the music is an acquired taste.  <P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sleeper||11-14-2001||10:13 PM||||if i'm in a good or bad mood, happy or sad, lazy or energetic. i like music that i can just come home from work and just push play and get a uplifting feeling. or albums i can listen to over and over without getting tired of listening to them. i can't remember too much of the albums i bought earlier in the year so these are the latest. <P>Incubus - Morning View<BR>P.O.D. - Satellite<BR>System of a Down - Toxicity<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||11-14-2001||10:38 PM||||My favorite bands of the year are Nickleback, Incubus, and Default. I also find myself listening to alot of my older tunes by the Door's and CCR. Overall, it has been a great year for tunes.<p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 11-14-2001).]||||reg||1
